  "limitations": [],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This theoretical paper applies the Reasonable Person Model (RPM) to aural architecture, proposing a framework for designing psychologically supportive acoustic environments. It argues that aural architecture, encompassing physical, perceptual, and cultural aspects of sound, significantly influences human information processing, including model-building, effectiveness, and meaningful actions. Key findings suggest that sound reflections and acoustic aesthetics contribute to understanding the environment, forming mental maps, and facilitating attention restoration by inducing involuntary fascination, thereby counteracting attention fatigue. The paper integrates Kaplan's preference-matrix (coherence, legibility, complexity, and mystery) to define preferred aural environments, asserting that such designs enhance well-being and productivity. The primary limitation is that this is a conceptual paper, synthesizing existing literature, and does not present new empirical data or specific design guidelines."
